ilege a  privilege privilege the nation entrusts  us with creating these complex  platforms that are going to take really  the most valuable resource we have which  is our young sailors and take them into  harm's way often deployed for months at  a time more than 5,000 people can live  aboard the Lincoln when it's fully  staffed that's a city it is a city with  its own Airport it's got all the things  that you need to accommodate 5,000  people in a city between food sleeping  arrangements entertainment training  every single thing that has to happen to  support the mission of the ship and then  you're going to go and drive this city  around out there at sea in an  environment that's actually pretty harsh  at some point the quality of our work is  going to get tested and it can't fail it  won't fail the man steering this massive  construction project was u.s. Navy  captain Ron Ravello before it came here  to Newport News you looked at like maps  right absolutely  you were using maps and and one of the  things you've done the last four years  is now this will all be navigated with  electronics Ravello said he'd like to  remind his crew what they're working  towards conceivably one of their sons or  daughters will be a member of the last  crew of this ship you know 25 years from  now you know that's how much life we're  putting back into this another  generation it's another generation of  sailors maybe even two to help recruit  and train qualified shipbuilders  Huntington Ingalls relies on its  apprentice school with an admissions  process as competitive as the Ivy League  students get paid to take classes while  getting hands-on experience and working  full-time toward a college degree these  four graduates span generations each had  a hand in getting the USS Lincoln ready  for duty it's spruill has been a  pipefitter at Newport News for 39 years  and his son now also works in the  shipyard  spruill help build the Lincoln and plans  to retire once the overhaul is complete  what does that mean to you to have been  able to kind of see that through there's  just something about this stage when  you're ready to go to see that when you  go down that River and you know all  those hours were put in and now you go  down on sea trials just a pride  what you do thirty-three-year-old  ramírez crumbly is an electrical Foreman  do you think that you feel that pride -  yes ma'am  I mean I didn't get it at first when I  first got here I mean I just started was  a job and then to actually complete a  carrier and to actually see it go out to  see I mean it's just outstanding and it  just makes you feel special as a person  that hey I was a part of that project  for medical and kristoff Lucas both  served aboard aircraft carriers in the  Navy it kind of makes you work a little  bit harder especially after knowing what  it's like to live on a ship all for  shipbuilders said working on equipment  that helps keep the country safe carries  with it a heightened sense of  responsibility in building ships with  the 50 year lifespan can quickly become  personal  my son is seven years old and he tells  me every day how he wants to be in the  military I mean in the Navy or something  like that I was 17 when I got in the  military and if he's if he follows in my  shoes he's gonna want to do the same  thing and if he's on a ship I want it to  be right because you could be working on  something now that he will be on  absolutely and that's that's something I  take very seriously now with the next  generation of carriers already being  built Huntington Ingalls industries  expected to be busy for decades to come  and to make sure that they have enough  shipbuilders to do the work CEO Mike  cutters actually donates his salary to  pay for preschool scholarships for the  children of his employees hoping they'll  follow their parents into the shipyard  Nora Oh Jan what a great story  you know aircraft carriers are the  largest projection of a nation's power  into 